> Template - copy this file to `~/.omo/rules/harness-findings.md` to install it as a live rule.

# Harness Findings - record what the harness got wrong

We continuously improve the harness we work in. Findings are only useful if they are
written down **when they happen**, in the repo that owns the harness - not left in a
session transcript that disappears.

**The log:** `docs/troubleshooting/harness-findings.md` (tracked, in the oh-my-openagent repo).

## WHEN TO APPEND

Append an entry when harness behaviour - OMO itself, its subagent/background-task
machinery, rules injection, tools, MCP servers, or the model runtime - does any of:

- **Blocks or strands work** - a session dies, a task hangs, a job is never collected.
- **Costs real time or tokens** - repeated runs, lost context, re-work, a wrong turn you
  had to back out of.
- **Reports something untrue** - a status line that disagrees with disk, a duration that
  froze, a success that did not happen.
- **Reliably misleads an agent** - instructions or messages that a competent agent reads
  the wrong way. This counts even when the harness behaved *correctly*: a predictable
  misreading is a harness finding, not user error.
- **Has no documented answer** - you had to read harness source to learn how something
  actually works.

Do NOT log ordinary product bugs in the repo you are working on. Those belong to that
project. This file is only for the tooling we execute *with*.

## HOW TO APPEND

1. Read the file's own "How to use this file" section and follow its entry template.
2. **Append only.** Never rewrite or delete an existing entry. If a finding turns out to
   be wrong or later gets fixed, add an `**Update:**` line to it - the history of what we
   believed and when is part of the value.
3. **Evidence over prose.** Paste the exact log line, status output, error text, or
   `file:line`. A finding nobody can reproduce is a rumour. Keep the narrative to a
   sentence or two.
4. **Label theory as theory.** If you do not know the root cause, write the hypothesis and
   say it is one. Never present a guess as a diagnosis.
5. **Verify before you write.** If you claim a mechanism, check it - read the harness
   source, confirm the config value, re-check the timestamps. A confidently wrong entry is
   worse than no entry, because the next agent will trust it.
6. Set `Severity` and `Area` from the tables in the file. Include the repo you were
   working in under `Observed in:` - the pattern across projects matters.

## COMMITTING

The findings log lives in a different repository from the work that produced the finding.
Commit it there, separately, on its own:

```bash
cd <path-to-your-oh-my-openagent-checkout>
git add docs/troubleshooting/harness-findings.md
git commit -m "docs(findings): <short title of the finding>"
```

Never fold this into an unrelated commit in the project you are working on, and never
`git add -A` in the harness repo - it frequently has unrelated work in progress.

If the oh-my-openagent checkout is dirty with someone else's changes, still commit **your one
file** by explicit path. Do not stash, rebase, or otherwise touch their work.

## WHAT NOT TO DO

- Do not interrupt the user's actual task to write a finding. Finish the work, then log it
  - or log it at a natural pause, such as while waiting on a long-running job.
- Do not log the same finding twice. Search the file first; if it is already there and you
  learned something new, append an `**Update:**` to the existing entry instead.
- Do not write speculative entries about behaviour you did not personally observe.
